      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1420",
      "html": "<ul><li>Scientific name: Gagata gagata (Hamilton, 1822)</li><li>Sometimes inhabits brackish water.</li><li>Breeding: unreported in the aquarium.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1420",
      "html": "<p>Asia: India, Bangladesh and Myanmar. Type locality: fresh water rivers and estuaries of Bengal.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1420",
      "html": "<p>Sometimes inhabits brackish water.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1420",
      "html": "<p>Identification: head and body silvery, without pattern. Fins, except caudal fin, black distally, with clear basal portions; caudal fin entirely clear. Dorsal spine extending well past adipose-fin origin when adpressed. Anal fin with 5-6 simple rays, 11 branched rays. Premaxilla with 4 rows of teeth. Most similar to G. melanopterus in coloration and having the highest gill raker counts among its congeners.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1420",
      "html": "<p>Unreported in the aquarium. There is no breeding report.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1420",
      "html": "<p>Etymology: the generic name comes from the local name of the fish (keyakatta). IUCN Red List: Least Concern (last assessed 2009).</p>"
    },
